 "anatomy-steps": {
  "card/card-1": {
   "steps": [
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What rises above the page?",
     "answer": "Only what floats",
     "why": "Only menus and dialogs float; cards use lines."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Elevation",
     "question": "What separates groups on the page?",
     "answer": "Lines",
     "why": "Thin lines guide the eye along rows."
    },
    {
     "kind": "answer",
     "scope": "Shape",
     "question": "What should control corners say?",
     "answer": "Slightly rounded",
     "why": "Small corners keep dense rows straight."
    },
    {
     "kind": "rule",
     "situation": "a card on the page",
     "use": "A hairline border, no shadow",
     "not": "a shadow",
     "why": "On a busy grid, lines separate. Shadows blur the edges."
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.surface.default",
     "key": "background",
     "value": "#FFFFFF",
     "darkValue": "#161B23",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "The base fill for content.",
     "pick": "neutral.0: the base for content; body text reads 17.28:1 on it, above the 4.5:1 floor.",
     "darkPick": "neutral.50: the base for content; body text reads 16.26:1 on it, above the 4.5:1 floor.",
     "shares": [
      "surface.raised",
      "action.secondary"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "radius.surface",
     "key": "radius",
     "value": "0.25rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "radius.4: We use a 4px corner for cards, menus and dialogs in order to make touching panels read as separate objects, in a precise tone by brand convention. A small radius does this by breaking the outline at each corner, just softer than the 2px controls. So panels read apart yet still tile neatly, because containers get their own corner (shape.roles, shape.count) and the brand is precise (brand.q1).",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "space.inset.container",
     "key": "padding",
     "value": "1rem",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "space.16",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "color.border.default",
     "key": "border",
     "value": "#DDE2EA",
     "darkValue": "#3D4655",
     "byScheme": true,
     "rule": "Quiet edges and dividers. Decorative, so it has no contrast minimum.",
     "pick": "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest 1.22:1, on surface.canvas). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum.",
     "darkPick": "neutral.200: a quiet line that stays visible on every surface it meets (lowest 1.46:1, on surface.raised). It decorates, so it holds no contrast minimum.",
     "shares": [
      "border.raised",
      "disabled.border"
     ]
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "border-width.default",
     "key": "border-width",
     "value": "1px",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "border-width.1",
     "shares": []
    },
    {
     "kind": "token",
     "semantic": "elevation.card",
     "key": "shadow",
     "value": "0px 0px 0px, #00000000",
     "byScheme": true,
     "pick": "shadow.none: We use no shadow for cards in order to keep them at the page's height: on the scale only floating layers rise. The missing shade does this by leaving the card flat, framed by its line. So the page reads as one flat grid, because only menus and dialogs float (elevation.roles) and shadows are subtle (elevation.values).",
     "shares": []
    }
   ]
  },
